# -*- mode: org; -*-

* 0.0.5-pre

** New features and UI changes

- The ~TodoWrite~ task list is no longer lost between turns.  Since the
  tool is marked ~:include nil~ (it would otherwise add a tool block to
  the transcript on every call), the model used to have no way of
  knowing the current task list once a new message was sent: the overlay
  would keep showing a frozen state while the model worked blind.

  The live list is now appended to the end of every outgoing request by
  a ~gptel-prompt-transform-functions~ handler
  (~gptel-agent--inject-todos~), the same mechanism gptel uses for
  context.  It never touches your buffer or the transcript, and since it
  is appended after the conversation it does not invalidate the cached
  prompt prefix.

  Injection is conditional on the ~TodoWrite~ tool being enabled for the
  request, so it costs nothing for agents that don't use it.  Two new
  options control it:
  - ~gptel-agent-todos-stale-after~ (default 3): after this many
    requests without a ~TodoWrite~ call, the injected list is marked
    ~STALE~ and the model is asked to update or drop it.  The task list
    overlay in the buffer marks the same condition.
  - ~gptel-agent-todos-max-injected~ (default 30): cap on items
    injected, to bound token cost.

- gptel-agent now works over TRAMP.  This means it can run processes on
  remote servers, grep and edit files and so on without the need to
  install Emacs or an agent harness.  This involves calling command line
  programs on the remote, so it requires a POSIX-y remote environment.

- New user option ~gptel-agent-preset~ to specify additional gptel
  settings for sub-agents.  This is a preset that applies to all
  sub-agent calls, before any agent-specific settings.

  Its main purpose is to allow using a cheaper/smaller (or larger!)
  model for sub-agent tasks, which is a common need depending on the
  estimated difficulty of such tasks.  Of course, this is a gptel preset
  so you are not restricted to specifying a backend/model -- any gptel
  setting is supported.

  It can be set to a preset name (a symbol) or a plist spec, for example:
  #+begin_src emacs-lisp
  (gptel-make-preset 'lite              ;elsewhere
    :backend "Gemini"
    :model 'gemini-flash-lite-latest)

  ;; Apply the "lite" preset to sub-agent calls
  (setq gptel-agent-preset 'lite)
  #+end_src
  or just
  #+begin_src emacs-lisp
  (setq gptel-agent-preset
        '(:backend "Gemini" :model gemini-flash-lite-latest))
  #+end_src

- Two new user options bound the cost of a runaway sub-agent task (the
  ~Agent~ tool):
  - ~gptel-agent-task-timeout~ (default 900 seconds): a sub-agent task
    that runs longer than this is failed.  Its overlay is removed, an
    error is returned to the calling model, and the request is aborted
    instead of lingering in the background.  Set it to ~nil~ to wait
    indefinitely.
  - ~gptel-agent-max-prompt-chars~ (default 600000): sub-agent prompts
    longer than this are refused up front, with an error telling the
    model to narrow the task or point the agent at files instead of
    pasting their contents in.  Set it to ~nil~ to disable the limit.

- New command ~gptel-agent-compact~ to compact/summarize a conversation
  and free up the context window.  It works on the buffer up to the
  cursor position or on the active region, so you can compact a part of
  a conversation if desired.  Currently this must be invoked manually,
  but auto-compaction is planned for the future.

  Be warned: this is lossy and modifies the chat buffer!

  The prompt used for compaction is customizable, see
  ~gptel-agent-compact-prompt~.  You can also specify additional
  compaction instructions interactively by calling ~gptel-agent-compact~
  with a prefix argument.

  This command is experimental, and the implementation of prompt
  compaction might change in the future.

** Notable bug fixes

- The tool limits are no longer ad hoc.  ~WebSearch~ and ~WebFetch~
  declare ~:timeout 30~ (the number their own URL timer was already
  using), the ~Agent~ tool declares ~:timeout~ as a function returning
  ~gptel-agent-task-timeout~ plus a minute, so that its own watchdog
  still fires first and customizing that option takes effect for the
  next call, and the ~Glob~ and ~Grep~ preview limit now follows
  ~gptel-tool-max-result-chars~ instead of a hardcoded 20000 characters.
  ~Bash~ declares ~:timeout~ of 120 seconds, so a command that hangs
  is caught in two minutes rather than in gptel's default ten, and a
  stale comment describing another client's 2/10 minute limits is gone.
  The model can raise the limit for a single call, see below.

- ~Bash~ commands are now stopped when gptel gives up on them.  The tool
  declares ~:cancel #'gptel-agent--cancel-bash~, so a command that
  outlives the per-call timeout -- or whose request is aborted -- is
  killed along with its output buffer, instead of running on in the
  background with nobody to read the result.  This needs gptel's
  ~:cancel~ support.

- The ~Glob~ tool was reworked.  It now
  - returns only regular files, so directory entries and the ~tree~
    report footer no longer leak into the results,
  - sorts all results by modification time, newest first.  The ~tree~
    backend previously sorted per directory only, so the ordering
    differed from the ~find~ backend's.
  - reports unreadable directories instead of silently dropping them.
    ~--prune~ used to hide ~tree~'s errors, making truncated results
    look like "no such file".
  - no longer needs GNU ~find~: ~-printf~ is gone, since sorting happens
    in Lisp.
  - validates ~depth~ (a positive integer or a numeric string) instead
    of forwarding ~0~ to ~tree~ and surfacing its usage error, and
    distinguishes a nonexistent path from an unreadable one.
  - states explicitly when nothing matched.

